            <ArticleTitle>Right coronary ostial atherectomy: a really close shave.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>225-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Percutaneous transluminal coronary balloon angioplasty (PTCA) has had limited success with higher complication and restenosis rates in aorto-ostial lesions. Directional coronary atherectomy (DCA) has been advocated as an alternative to PTCA in such lesions. In this report, we describe a potential complication of DCA in right coronary ostial lesions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
